# Corporate Network Security: Automated Malicious Traffic Sensor (IDS)

## Executive Summary
This project demonstrates the deployment, configuration, and validation of an open-source Network Intrusion Detection System (IDS) using Suricata integrated within a virtualized pfSense firewall ecosystem. The primary objective was to establish a resilient network perimeter capable of identifying, categorizing, and logging real-time reconnaissance and web-exploitation traffic launched from an adversarial machine (Kali Linux) targeting a corporate network infrastructure.

## Network Topology & Lab Architecture
The infrastructure is completely virtualized and isolated within a host-only virtual network chokepoint, forcing all traffic to pass through the security gateway's deep packet inspection (DPI) engine.

```text
                  [ WAN / Public Internet ]
                             |
                             v
           +-----------------------------------+

           |         pfSense Firewall          |
           |   - Adapter 1 (WAN): NAT          |
           |   - Adapter 2 (LAN): 10.10.10.1   |
           +-----------------------------------+
                             |
            +----------------+----------------+

            | (Isolated Layer 2 Virtual Wire) |
            |                                 |
            v                                 v
   +------------------+             +--------------------+

   |    Kali Linux    |             |  Windows 11 Client |
   | Attacker Node    |             | Workstation Target |
   | IP: 10.10.10.100 |             | IP: 10.10.10.10    |
   +------------------+             +--------------------+
```

## Core Objectives
1. **Network Segmentation:** Establish strict network zones using a dual-homed firewall to ensure secure egress/ingress traffic routes.
2. **Deep Packet Inspection Engine:** Deploy and configure the Suricata binary to monitor the private LAN broadcast domain in promiscuous mode.
3. **Threat Intelligence Feed Integration:** Synchronize the sensor with the Emerging Threats (ET) Open community rule definitions.
4. **Adversarial Validation:** Execute controlled, multi-stage attack vectors from the Kali Linux host to test sensor fidelity and verify threat classification metrics.

